## 3. Set — Complete Guide

A Set is an **unordered** collection of **unique** elements. No duplicates allowed. No index access. Extremely efficient for membership checks and deduplication.

### 3.1 Key Characteristics

- **Unordered** — the iteration order is not guaranteed and may differ from insertion order
- **No duplicates** — adding a duplicate is silently ignored (no error)
- **No index access** — cannot use `get(0)`, `set(1,x)`, etc.
- **Internally uses hashing** — very fast O(1) for `contains()` checks

---

### 3.2 Three Ways to Create a Set

```apex
// Way 1: Empty Set
Set<String> colorsSet = new Set<String>();

// Way 2: With initial values — duplicates are auto-removed
Set<String> colorsSet = new Set<String>{'Red','Black','Blue','Green','Black'};
// Result: {Red, Black, Blue, Green} — 'Black' duplicate silently dropped

// Way 3: Declare only
Set<String> colorsSet;
colorsSet = new Set<String>();

// Common real-world pattern: collect unique IDs
Set<Id> accountIdSet = new Set<Id>();
for (Contact c : contactList) {
    accountIdSet.add(c.AccountId);  // auto-deduplication!
}
```

---

### 3.3 All Key Set Methods

| Method | Description & Example |
|--------|----------------------|
| **`add(element)`** | Adds element. Silently ignored if duplicate. `colorsSet.add('Yellow');` |
| **`contains(element)`** | Returns true/false. `colorsSet.contains('Red');` → `true` |
| **`remove(value)`** | Removes specific element. `colorsSet.remove('Blue');` |
| **`addAll(set or list)`** | Adds all elements from another collection. `set1.addAll(list1);` |
| **`removeAll(set or list)`** | Removes all elements found in given collection. |
| **`retainAll(set or list)`** | Keeps only elements that exist in both (intersection). |
| **`size()`** | Returns total unique element count. `colorsSet.size();` |
| **`isEmpty()`** | Returns true if empty. `colorsSet.isEmpty();` |
| **`clear()`** | Removes all elements. `colorsSet.clear();` |
| **`clone()`** | Returns shallow copy of the set. |
| **`equals(anotherSet)`** | Returns true if both sets contain same elements. |

---

### 3.4 Hands-On Code Walkthrough

```apex
// Deduplication in action
Set<String> colors = new Set<String>{'black','Red','Black','Blue','Green','Black'};
System.debug(colors);               // {black, Red, Black, Blue, Green} — 2 duplicates removed
System.debug(colors.contains('Black')); // true (case-sensitive!)
System.debug(colors.size());        // 5 unique values

// Adding from another Set
Set<String> fruits = new Set<String>();
fruits.add('Apple'); fruits.add('Banana'); fruits.add('Mango');
colors.addAll(fruits);
System.debug(colors);  // all colors + fruits combined

// Real-world: Convert Set to List for DML
List<Account> accountList = new List<Account>();
accountList.addAll(accountSet);  // Set → List for insert
insert accountList;
```

---

### 3.5 When to Use a Set

- Uniqueness must be guaranteed — record IDs, email addresses, phone numbers
- Fast membership check — "does this ID already exist?" using `contains()`
- Deduplication before SOQL `WHERE IN` queries
- Collecting parent IDs from a list of child records in triggers

> 📌 To use a Set in a SOQL `WHERE IN` clause, you can pass it directly: `[SELECT Id FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIdSet]` — no need to convert to List first.

