## 5. The Map Pattern — Governor Limit Optimization

The **single most important** Collection pattern in Salesforce development. Using a Map to eliminate SOQL queries inside loops is tested in almost every Apex interview.

### ✗ Bad Pattern — SOQL Inside Loop *(Governor Limit Violation)*

```apex
// WRONG — This hits governor limit: 101 SOQL queries for 101 contacts
List<Contact> contacts = [SELECT Id, AccountId FROM Contact LIMIT 200];

for (Contact c : contacts) {
    // SOQL INSIDE LOOP — each iteration = 1 query = DANGER
    Account acc = [SELECT Id, Name, Phone FROM Account WHERE Id = :c.AccountId];
    System.debug(acc.Name);
}

// If contacts has 101+ records → System.LimitException: Too many SOQL queries: 101
```

### ✓ Correct Pattern — `Map<Id, SObject>` Outside Loop

```apex
// CORRECT — Only 2 SOQL queries regardless of record count

// Step 1: Collect all parent IDs into a Set (unique, no duplicates)
List<Contact> contacts = [SELECT Id, AccountId, Name FROM Contact LIMIT 200];
Set<Id> accountIdSet = new Set<Id>();
for (Contact c : contacts) {
    accountIdSet.add(c.AccountId);
}

// Step 2: Single SOQL → store in Map for O(1) lookup
Map<Id, Account> accountMap = new Map<Id, Account>(
    [SELECT Id, Name, Phone FROM Account WHERE Id IN :accountIdSet]
);

// Step 3: Loop and lookup from Map — NO more SOQL inside loop
for (Contact c : contacts) {
    Account acc = accountMap.get(c.AccountId);  // instant lookup
    if (acc != null) {
        System.debug('Contact: ' + c.Name + ' | Account: ' + acc.Name);
    }
}

// Result: Only 2 total SOQL queries — scales to any record count!
```

> 📌 This pattern is the foundation of bulkified Apex triggers. In any interview, when asked **"how do you avoid governor limits in a trigger?"**, this is the answer.

---

## 6. Collections — Full Comparison Reference

| Feature | List | Set | Map |
|---------|:----:|:---:|:---:|
| **Interface** | `List<T>` | `Set<T>` | `Map<K,V>` |
| **Order** | Insertion order | Random/hash order | Random/hash order |
| **Duplicates** | Allowed | Not allowed | Keys: No, Values: Yes |
| **Null values** | Allowed | One null allowed | Key: one null, Value: any |
| **Index access** | Yes — `get(i)`, `set(i,v)` | No | By key — `get(key)` |
| **Membership check** | `contains(el)` — O(n) | `contains(el)` — O(1) | `containsKey(k)` — O(1) |
| **DML operations** | Required — `insert List` | Convert to List first | Get `.values()` as List |
| **SOQL return type** | Yes — always a `List` | No | `Map<Id,SObj>` constructor |
| **Memory overhead** | Lowest | Medium | Highest |
| **Best use case** | Ordered data, SOQL results | Unique IDs, dedup | Key-value lookup, triggers |

